"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[7784],{7784:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-01","slug":"base-01","trackId":"base","order":2,"title":"Blockchain and Cryptocurrency","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":5,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-06"],"markdown":"Lesson 1 \xb7 4 min\\n\\n## Blockchain\\n\\nWhat blockchain and cryptocurrency are\\n\\nAt its simplest, cryptocurrency is a digital asset that can be stored and transferred through a special network called a blockchain. To understand why such a system was needed, compare it with ordinary money.\\n\\n### Crypto vs Ordinary Money\\n\\nWhen you use ordinary money, an intermediary almost always stands between you and another person. A transfer to a friend passes through a bank. Paying by card in a shop involves a bank and a payment network. Receiving a salary also involves banking infrastructure.\\n\\nBanks keep the records showing how much money is in your account and what transactions have taken place. They can also impose limits, reject transactions or block account access in certain situations. Cryptocurrencies proposed a different approach.\\n\\nInstead of a single bank holding the entire transaction history, a network of participants jointly maintains that history under predetermined rules. Cryptocurrency can therefore be transferred directly between two network participants without a bank having to act as intermediary. This is one of the central ideas of cryptocurrency:\\n\\nallowing people to transfer value to one another through an open network, rather than only through centralized financial institutions.\\n\\n### Blockchain as a Shared Notebook\\n\\nImagine a large shared notebook. It records that one person sent another a certain amount of cryptocurrency. A new transfer creates a new entry. The essential difference is that this notebook is not kept in a single bank’s vault.\\n\\nMany network participants store and verify copies of the transaction history. When a new transaction arrives, the network must agree that it follows the rules. The entry is then added to the shared history. This shared transaction history is what we call a blockchain.\\n\\nIn effect, a blockchain lets many strangers use the same system and agree on which transactions actually occurred. They do not have to trust one particular bank or company.\\n\\nTap to zoom in\\n\\n### Why It Is Called Blockchain\\n\\nThe word blockchain literally means a “chain of blocks.” Transactions are grouped into blocks. Once one block is filled and confirmed, another follows it. This creates a sequence:\\n\\nBlock 1 → Block 2 → Block 3 → Block 4\\n\\nEach new block is linked to the preceding block. The entire transaction history therefore forms one continuous chain. Old entries cannot simply be rewritten retrospectively without detection. Changing information in a previous block breaks its links to subsequent blocks, and other network participants see the inconsistency.\\n\\nThis is why blockchain information is considered highly resistant to alteration.\\n\\nTry quietly rewriting an old entry\\n-\\n-\\n-\\n- The blocks are linked together. Tap any block except the last and add an extra zero to your balance.\\n\\n### Who Maintains This Notebook?\\n\\nA reasonable question is:\\n\\nif there is no single bank, who decides which transactions are valid? The network does. Participants check new transactions against established rules and reach agreement about which can be added to the blockchain. Different blockchains organize this process differently.\\n\\nWe do not need the technical details yet. The general principle matters:\\n\\nno single person or organization decides which version of history is correct; the network’s rules and participants determine that.\\n\\n### What an Ordinary Transfer Looks Like\\n\\nConsider a simple example. Masha wants to send cryptocurrency to Sasha. She creates a transfer. Information about the transaction is submitted to the network.\\n\\nThe network checks whether the transfer complies with its rules. If everything is valid, the transaction is confirmed and added to the blockchain. The network now has an updated history:\\n\\nMasha sent Sasha a certain amount of cryptocurrency. Think of it as another line in the shared notebook. Instead of a single bank, a distributed network maintains this history simultaneously.\\n\\n### Why Any of This Is Necessary\\n\\nIn the ordinary financial system, we constantly trust intermediaries. We trust a bank to calculate the money in our account correctly. We trust a payment network to execute a transfer. We trust the infrastructure handling our transactions.\\n\\nBlockchain proposed another approach. Instead of relying on one intermediary, a system can have rules known in advance and a transaction history confirmed by a network. This does not mean blockchain is automatically better than banking in every respect. Each model has advantages, disadvantages and risks.\\n\\nBut blockchain made it possible for the first time to create digital assets that people can transfer over the internet without a bank necessarily participating. That is how cryptocurrencies emerged. Bitcoin is the best-known example. Thousands of other cryptocurrencies and blockchains followed, each with its own purposes and rules.\\n\\nThe basic idea remains the same:\\n\\na blockchain maintains a shared transaction history without one central owner, and cryptocurrency is one type of digital asset that can exist and move within such a system.\\n\\nThe next article covers security. Security is the most important task in crypto.\\n\\n### Check Yourself 1 / 2\\n\\nWhat is the main difference between blockchain and an ordinary bank database?\\n\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Blockchain** — a record of all transactions that everyone can inspect: entries can be added, but old entries cannot be changed. It is stored on millions of computers simultaneously, making falsification of the history physically impossible.\\n\\n### End-of-Lesson Quiz (“Check Yourself”):\\n**Question 1: What is the main difference between blockchain and an ordinary bank database?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: The blockchain is stored only on the cryptocurrency owner’s computer\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: A network of participants maintains the transaction history, rather than one central bank\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Any old transaction can be deleted from the blockchain\\n\\n**Question 2: What happens after a transaction enters the blockchain?**\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 1: It becomes part of the network’s shared transaction history\\n- ⚪ Option 2: It is automatically deleted after a few days\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Blockchain support can reverse it\\n\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-01-1","prompt":"What is the main difference between blockchain and an ordinary bank database?","options":[{"id":"base-01-1-1","label":"The blockchain is stored only on the cryptocurrency owner’s computer","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. A network of participants maintains the transaction history, rather than one central bank"},{"id":"base-01-1-2","label":"A network of participants maintains the transaction history, rather than one central bank","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. A network of participants maintains the transaction history, rather than one central bank"},{"id":"base-01-1-3","label":"Any old transaction can be deleted from the blockchain","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. A network of participants maintains the transaction history, rather than one central bank"}],"explanation":"A network of participants maintains the transaction history, rather than one central bank","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:312–315 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-01-2","prompt":"What happens after a transaction enters the blockchain?","options":[{"id":"base-01-2-1","label":"It becomes part of the network’s shared transaction history","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. It becomes part of the network’s shared transaction history"},{"id":"base-01-2-2","label":"It is automatically deleted after a few days","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. It becomes part of the network’s shared transaction history"},{"id":"base-01-2-3","label":"Blockchain support can reverse it","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. It becomes part of the network’s shared transaction history"}],"explanation":"It becomes part of the network’s shared transaction history","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:317–320 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-01-3","prompt":"Why does changing one record break verification of the chain of blocks?","options":[{"id":"base-01-3-1","label":"The changed block’s hash no longer matches the reference stored in the next block","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. The next block stores the preceding block’s hash; substitution breaks that verifiable link."},{"id":"base-01-3-2","label":"The wallet automatically returns all transfers to their senders","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"No. No reverse transfer occurs; nodes detect the hash mismatch."},{"id":"base-01-3-3","label":"Old records can be changed freely without leaving a trace","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"No. Changing data changes the hash and breaks the link to the next block."}],"explanation":"Each block references the preceding block through its hash. Verification detects data substitution; the resilience of a real network also depends on its consensus rules.","sourceRef":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md:254–299; source-grounded addition to the 86-question specification table"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":221,"lineEnd":325}}')}}]);